<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ziya Gökalp</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1876">1876</a>–<a href="/years/1924">1924</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Mehmet Ziya Gökalp, Mehmed Ziya</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ziya Gökalp was a Turkish sociologist, writer, poet and politician, born in Diyarbakır in 1876, who is considered the father of Turkish nationalism. A chief ideologist of the Committee of Union and Progress, he drew on Émile Durkheim to reject Ottomanism and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamism">Islamism</a> in favour of Turkish nationalism, advocated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkification">Turkification</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> and regarded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> as an undesirable foreign body in the national state; many historians have suggested that his nationalism contributed to the Armenian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a>. Exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a> from 1919 to 1921, he wrote The Principles of Turkism (1923), and his ideas shaped the reforms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mustafa-kemal-ataturk">Mustafa Kemal Atatürk</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ziya-gokalp.jpg" alt="Ziya Gökalp" width="330" height="436" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown photographer Unknown photographer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ziya_Gökalp-1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
